Id: no-random
Pattern: (\bnew[[:space:]]+Random\(|\bRandom\.Shared|\bGuid\.NewGuid\(|\bGuid\.CreateVersion7\(|\bPath\.GetRandomFileName\()
Subject: *.cs
Except: :!*SystemRandomSource.cs
Issue: 47

The same argument as the clock, for the other source of an answer a test cannot
predict. A shuffled shelf, a jittered backoff and a generated identifier are all
things a test has to be able to fix, and it can only fix them if they come from
somewhere it can supply.

The exception is the one implementation of IRandomSource, which #47 added, and
it is written as a file suffix for the reason on no-wall-clock. Nothing behind
it is cryptographic: a key or a token needs a generator chosen for that job and
this rule does not stand in for that choice.


TWO SPELLINGS OF AN UNPREDICTABLE ANSWER ARE HERE AND THE FIRST THREE DID NOT
REACH THEM. Guid.CreateVersion7 is the runtime's newer identifier and this
project builds on the framework that added it, so a reader who knows the type
reaches for it rather than for NewGuid, and a rule naming only NewGuid was
silent on the same draw one method along. The place it would arrive is a want
identifier or an item identity, which is exactly where #99 says the value has to
be derived and stable rather than generated.

Path.GetRandomFileName is the same defect wearing a filename. A store that
writes through a name a test cannot predict cannot be asserted against, and the
alternative is already in the tree: the catalogue writes through a fixed suffix
and moves it into place, so nothing here needs a drawn name.

A CRYPTOGRAPHIC GENERATOR IS DELIBERATELY NOT REFUSED. The sentence above says
this rule does not stand in for that choice, and refusing
RandomNumberGenerator would refuse the one draw a key or a token is supposed to
be made with, which is #77 and #80 rather than this. What that leaves is a bound
rather than a gap: a value drawn that way is unpredictable to a test in exactly
the way this rule is otherwise about, and nothing here refuses it.
